# CODEOWNERS — who is asked to review changes to what.
#
# GitHub reads this file and automatically requests a review from the listed
# owner when a pull request touches a matching path. It is a routing table, not
# an access-control mechanism: it decides who gets *asked*, not who *can* merge.
# Enforcement would come from a branch-protection rule requiring code-owner
# review, which is a repository setting, not something this file can do.
#
# MAINTAINERS: as additional maintainers are given commit access, add them to
# the relevant lines below and to the table in GOVERNANCE.md. Listing two or
# more owners on a path is what lets a change be reviewed by someone other than
# its author — the single most useful thing this file can be made to do, and
# the specific gap recorded under the Community criterion in
# docs/security/cisa-oss-2026-conformance.md.
#
# Syntax: <path pattern>  <@owner> [@owner ...]
# Later matching lines win, so the general rule comes first.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default owner for everything not matched below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*                               @ejosterberg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security-sensitive surfaces. Changes here need a deliberate second look:
# authentication, the permission system, session handling, encryption, and the
# audit trail. A mistake in this set is not a bug, it is an incident.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
